.TH ELECTRUM\-READ 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.2.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
electrum\-read \- Electrum wallet file (JSON, optionally field\-encrypted, or BIE1 whole\-file encrypted) \-> its seed phrase
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
electrum\-read < (password line, then Electrum wallet JSON) \-> {"mnemonic"} / {"xprv"}
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
({"mnemonic"}) and/or master key ({"xprv"}). Output CONTAINS SECRETS. Pipe to seed\-to\-master
(mnemonic) or derive (xprv). stdin: line 1 = wallet password (empty if none), rest = wallet file.
Electrum 1.x files: the keys themselves ({"wif","address","path"}, uncompressed P2PKH, `\-\-count N`
per branch, default 20), checked against the file's master_public_key.
Thin wrapper around blocktools_secrets::electrum.
.SH OPTIONS
No flags. Input is read from stdin or the arguments shown in SYNOPSIS.
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
electrum\-read: Electrum wallet file (JSON, optionally field\-encrypted, or BIE1 whole\-file encrypted) \-> its seed phrase
({"mnemonic"}) and/or master key ({"xprv"}). Output CONTAINS SECRETS. Pipe to seed\-to\-master
(mnemonic) or derive (xprv). stdin: line 1 = wallet password (empty if none), rest = wallet file.
Thin wrapper around blocktools_secrets::electrum.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
last status ok
.TP
1
last status wrong_password
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ { echo btcr\-test\-password; cat "$R/fixtures/external/electrum28\-wallet"; } | electrum\-read
# exit 0, last status: ok
$ { echo wrong; cat "$R/fixtures/external/electrum28\-wallet"; } | electrum\-read
# exit 1, last status: wrong_password
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
It may open hostile wallet files and handle secrets: run it sealed and offline (./blocktools run; sealed\-run allows it).
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
